<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasekura Tsunenaga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1571">1571</a>–<a href="/years/1622">1622</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasekura Tsunenaga was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> in the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> who led a Japanese embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from 1613 to 1620. Sailing via <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, he met King Philip III and was received by Pope Paul V in 1615. By the time he returned, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the mission brought no lasting results.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasekura-tsunenaga.jpg" alt="Hasekura Tsunenaga" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archita Ricci, formerly attributed to Claude Déruet Claude Deruet[1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hasekura_in_Rome.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hasekura Rokuemon Tsunenaga was born in 1571 in Okitama district, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamagata-prefecture">Yamagata Prefecture</a>. His father was Yamaguchi Tsuneshige, and the family claimed ancestral ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a>. A mid-level noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the Sendai Domain, he served the daimyō <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a> directly and received an annual stipend of 1200 koku. He was of roughly the same age as Masamune, who entrusted him with several important missions as his representative. In 1597 he served for six months as a samurai in the Japanese invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Franciscan friar Luis Sotelo persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a> to send a representative to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, and a Japanese embassy was planned to accompany the explorer Sebastián Vizcaíno back there. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a>, the San Juan Bautista, was built in 45 days, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/date-masamune">Date Masamune</a>, in charge of the project, named Hasekura to lead the mission. It left on 28 October 1613 with around 180 people, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a>, merchants, sailors and some 40 Spaniards and Portuguese. It reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> on 25 January 1614 after three months at sea, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in March. In July the embassy changed ships in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, and on 5 October 1614 it arrived at Sanlúcar de Barrameda.</p><p>Hasekura entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> on 23 October 1614 and met King Philip III in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 30 January 1615, delivering Masamune's letter and an offer of a treaty. Bad weather held the embassy at Saint-Tropez on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 20 September 1615, and on 3 November Hasekura gave Pope Paul V two gilded letters, requesting a trade treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and missionaries for Japan. In April 1616 the king declined a trade agreement, since the embassy did not appear to be an official one from the ruler of Japan, who had ordered missionaries expelled in January 1614.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hasekura left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in June 1616, crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> again in 1619, and sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> in August 1620. Japan had changed: persecution of Christians had begun in 1614, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Ieyasu</a> had died in 1616, and the country was moving towards isolation. Two days after his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sendai">Sendai</a>, an edict ordered Christians there to abandon their faith. What became of Hasekura is unknown; rumours said that he abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, was martyred, or practised in secret. His son Tsuneyori was beheaded in 1640 for failing to denounce Christians in his household.</p><p>His journey was forgotten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> until the Iwakura mission was shown documents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1873. Statues of him stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, Coria del Río and several places in Japan. About 700 inhabitants of Coria del Río bear the surname Japón, identifying them as descendants of members of his delegation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hasekura Tsunenaga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Masamune developed rice land on the Sendai plain and shipped surplus rice to Edo, and in 1613 he sent <b>Hasekura Tsunenaga</b> on an embassy to Spain and Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/sendai-domain">Sendai Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nagai manor. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsu-province">Mutsu Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.86,141.17&amp;year=1604" aria-label="Mutsu Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Hasekura Tsunenaga"><image href="/maps/globes/g150n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4414 4222h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4414 4222h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=38.86,141.17&amp;year=1604">What was here in 1604? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
